# homma

homma orchestrates a multi-repository workspace: it knows which repositories the
workspace contains, who works in it, where each participant's work happens, and
how they correspond.

It is deliberately small. Designing and building inside a repository belongs to
that repository's own tooling; homma's job is what spans repositories and what is
true of the workspace rather than of any one member.

## What it must be

**General.** homma runs against a workspace it has never seen. **No key is
required**: the content repository defaults to the configuration's own directory,
and every path has a default homma will create plus an override for a workspace
whose convention differs. So a fresh directory with a registry in it is already a
workspace. Nothing about the workspace homma was born in is compiled in, which is
why layout, roles and record kinds are data rather than code.

Membership follows from that. A directory under the workspace root that is its
own repository is a member, and nothing lists it. Which forge it lives on and
who owns it come from its `origin` remote, which is the thing that decides
where a push lands and is therefore the only honest source for either. A
workspace that renames a repository, clones a new one, or drops one is correct
the moment the tree is, with nothing left saying otherwise.

**Minimal, and delegating.** Where a member repository has its own tooling, homma
drives it rather than reimplementing it. Releasing is the case where the tooling
is the workspace's own: a release is a merge of `dev` onto `main` tagged,
published and announced as one act, gated by a run on the developer's machine
that the forge is told about, and homma drives that because it is the one thing
every workspace has at hand.

## The four primitives

Everything else is a view over these, a kind declared in configuration, or prose
in a generated file.

**The record store.** One engine for everything homma persists. Kinds declare
what attributes a record has and whether it may be edited, so correspondence and
structural work share a write path and differ by a flag.

**The daemon.** Sole writer of the store, sole waker of participants, sole arbiter
of who may act on whose behalf. Three verbs: append a record, deliver what is
waiting, grant a dispatch.

**The registry.** Identity as configuration rather than as a process, so a
participant outlives whatever was running it.

**The generation pass.** Configuration and templates into definitions, settings,
hooks and links; the sole writer of the agent configuration in every workspace it
manages.

## The layers

```
L0 contracts     types and traits, no I/O
L1 mechanism     git, forge, filesystem. things with side effects
L2 store         records and their persistence
L3 domain        registry, correspondence, work
L4 service       the daemon and its surfaces
L5 surface       what a human or an agent drives
```

Crates arrive when something needs them. An empty crate holding a name is a
directory pretending to be a decision.

## What exists

`homma-api` carries the vocabulary. `homma-store` persists records.
`homma-org` holds the registry, the workspace lifecycle and generation.
`homma-engine` is the command surface, and `homma` is the launcher that finds
the workspace, reads the version of the engine it pins, and runs that one.
`homma-core` still holds forge clients and git operations in a pre-adoption
split that a later round dissolves, and it is where the release lives: the gate
that measures a commit, the run record and commit status it leaves, and the
merge, tag, changelog, GitHub release and registry publish that carry `dev`
onto `main`. `homma release` in `homma-engine` is its surface, and
`CORE_RELEASE.md` is the whole of it.

## The command surface

```
homma org      who exists
homma work     workspaces and what is in them
homma sauna    correspondence and work
homma serve    the daemon and its surfaces
homma release  the gate, the run record, and dev onto main as a release
```

A call with a terminal attached should print for a human, and a call without one
should print its result and nothing else, because something is parsing it. That
split is decided and **not yet built**: nothing in the tree senses a terminal, and
`homma org` prints the same way either way.

Two commands read and write the shared tool configs described in the org
design. `homma repo config check` compares a repository against them and reports;
it exits non-zero when something is missing that the repository is meant to have.
`homma repo config init` places what is missing. They are two commands rather
than one because the check runs on a path that must not write, and placing a
config is a deliberate act.

Which non-zero it is carries the rest of the answer, and the split belongs to
the binary rather than to these two commands. Zero is a command that ran with
nothing to say, `1` is one that ran and reported, and `2` is one that could not
run at all, which is what any error reaching the entry point returns. A caller
has no other way to ask, and the commonest thing behind a `2` here is the
workspace manifest failing to parse, which is nobody's repository's fault.

That path is the gate. A workspace-level hook already stands in front of
`git commit` and `git push` from inside any member repository, works out which
repository the call came from, and refuses when that repository's mockspace
bootstrap is incomplete. It gains the config check, and refuses the same way,
naming the command that fixes it where there is one. Where the check could not
run it says that instead, and names the workspace manifest, because sending
somebody to a command that fails the same way is worse than saying nothing.

It lives there rather than in a per-repository git hook because mockspace owns
`core.hooksPath` in every repository it manages, and a second owner of one hook
path is a fight neither wins. A workspace hook is also one file in one place,
which matters because the per-repository wiring is exactly the thing that can be
quietly absent.

The gate asks homma rather than deciding for itself, so the rule that says what
a repository owes exists once. It refuses when it cannot ask at all, for the
same reason it refuses when it cannot read its input: a check that could not run
must never be indistinguishable from one that ran and found nothing.

## What a status answers

`homma status` answers what state the workspace is in. That question is best
served by what is wrong rather than by an inventory of what is fine, so it covers
every population homma can report and shows, by default, only what is not in the
state it should be in. A repository with nothing to say about it takes one line.
`--full` prints every population whole, for when that is what somebody wants.

The narrower verbs are unchanged and stay the place to see one population
entirely, healthy entries included. What the plain status buys is not having to
know which of them to reach for.

Covered: the workspace and its forges, the agent surfaces including whether the
repository's git hooks are wired at all, the shared tool configs, and the working
tree. The doc surfaces are deliberately not, being a survey of what a repository
has rather than a report of anything being wrong, and nothing offline is left
out for cost. Nothing here reaches the network, which is what makes it the
command to run first.

The machine-readable document grows fields rather than changing shape, so what
reads it today keeps working and simply ignores what it does not know. It is
never summarised, whatever the human output is doing: a document that quietly
omitted the healthy members would be misdescribing the population to the one
reader with no way to notice.
